### applying max missing 0.3 (it is 0.6 in the workshop) ###
Important interpretation
--max-missing refers to the fraction of individuals with NON-missing data.
So:
Filter	                       Meaning
--max-missing 0.9	    SNP present in ≥90% individuals
--max-missing 0.8	    SNP present in ≥80% individuals
--max-missing 0.6	    SNP present in ≥60% individuals
Higher value = stricter filtering.

## Biological tradeoff ##
Lower threshold (0.6)
Pros: keeps more SNPs, useful for sparse ddRAD data

Cons: more missing data/noise, may affect PCA/ADMIXTURE

Higher threshold (0.9)
Pros: cleaner dataset, more reliable SNPs

Cons: many SNPs removed, may lose population signal

## Your plot is useful for choosing this ##
Your variant_plot.png likely shows:
SNP count drops sharply as max-missing becomes stricter.
That curve helps choose a balance between: data quality, SNP retention.

